    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. regarding the bitumen viscosity part, the Phoenix heat is pretty extreme here. do you think the binder is reaching its softening point too early, or is it more likely a matter of the bitumen becoming too brittle during the cooler desert nights? also, how much does subgrade compaction play into the longitudinal cracking specifically?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The extreme heat in Phoenix can cause bitumen to soften prematurely, leading to a unsupported and weaker pavement system. However, during cooler desert nights, the asphalt binder can become brittle due to significant temperature drops, causing it to lose its impact resistance and become more prone to cracking. If the subgrade is poorly compacted or has low stability, it can allow settlement and movement under traffic loads, which can then reflect into longitudinal cracking.",
      "source": "target",
